    "media:imagetext": "Penciled\nnote - Please\nexcuse this narrative but I had to write it\nWLB\n-5-\nflat power glide with his co-pilot hanging out of the window watching the\n; river and loon peering ahend for sempans or junks. When they seemed to\nbe about eight or ten feet off the water the co-pilot yelled and Moon\npulled back on his throttles and eased back on his stick and shut his eyes\nand prayed to Buddha.\nThe next morning he took off for Ichang where he\nLanded and filled up\nhis plane with passengers we had left there and returned\nto Chungking•\nit is over.\nno one could keep that up long• I don't see how they accomplished as much as\n2 or no arcade ty or 1r hang but\nthey did. I think the Minister van plessed.\nI would like to say that Allison also flew to Hankow every night\nThe firet night there was nothing for him to do so he\nreturned to Chungking at day break bringing some of NAC's staff, a lot of\nmeil and pay passengers for CA. The second night, he brought gone aviation\nand other officiols and mall for CNAC. The third night after his return\nfrom Hengyang with Sharp, he brought fourteen pay passengers to Chungking for\nCNAC after spending one night en route at Ichang.\nHe then told me be was\nordered to fly to Kunming on the 29th and would be glad to take passengers\nbut unfortunately his orders were chonged and he did not go to Kunming.\nI don't know if you are still reading this or it you have\nconsigned it to the waste basket where it rightly belongs. I think we may say\nthat one period of the war is ending, and the tuture may be different.\ncertainiy looks bad for China now but actually it seems to me it is not waally\nas bad as it was after the fall of Manking nearly a year ago.\nChinese are worse off but so are the Japanese, much worse off. They are\ngetting further from their base, apparently they are realling beginning to feel\nthe pinch, and they have no more big important towns or objectives they can\nwork towards with the feeling that if they can take that, their situstion will\nImprove.\nAlao the Japanese are undoubtedly beginning to\nweaken thenselves at\nhome and they ere getting themselves into a very vulnerable position for sone\nother nation to take advantage of. If the Chinese will juat hold on I believe\nthey have a good chance to get very favourable peace terms. I think the\nGeneraliasino will certainly keep up the fight.\nIt all depends on how China\nunites behind him. I have been saying for a long time that we should Inow\nsomething definite in sixty days.\nAgain I think we should. It may not be\ngood nows. It may be a bad pesce for China or it nay be the cortain knowledge\nthat China will keep on fighting to the bitter end but whatever the future has\nIn store should be developed in sixty daye I think.\nIt is not a very pleasant outlook but at lesst CNAC is still\nfairly well organized and is7going concern with a little reserve. I think\nCNAC will probabiy go back into the Red again before long but by doing a lot\nof drastie reorganizing when it becomes necessary we will manage to hold on.\nCHAC takes a lot of licking.\nYours very truly,\nCopy Mr. Bixby\nWEB: SPJ\n(sgd.) 1. Le BOND\nPenciled note - Our Ford Station wagon and mechanics got to Ichang safely.",
